      "content": "That makes sense. It seems like the burden of proof is heavily on the current owner to show it wasn't just a matter of convenience.\n\nOne thing I'm looking at in the files is the language regarding \"nuisance\" and \"infringement.\" If we argue that this encroachment is an \"encroaching nuisance\" because it restricts the legal utility of the land, does the city treat that differently than a standard setback variance? I want to know if there's any administrative precedent where a \"rogue\" or \"unauthorized\" structural element is treated as a separate entity in the hearing, almost as if the city treats the violation itself as a persistent, unwanted presence that must be dealt with before the primary application can even proceed.",
