    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The language regarding \"nuisance\" and \"infringement\" is indeed an important aspect of the case. In Austin, a nuisance is generally defined as a condition or activity that interferes with the use or enjoyment of a property, and can be considered a separate entity from the underlying land use issue.\n\nIn the context of a pre-existing masonry wall, if the city views the wall as an \"encroaching nuisance,\" it may consider the wall to be a persistent, unwanted presence that must be addressed before the primary application can proceed. This approach is often referred to as a \"nuisance-based\" approach, where the city treats the violation itself as a separate issue that must be resolved before the main application can be considered.\n\nAdministratively, this means that the city may consider the wall to be a \"rogue\" or \"unauthorized\" element that must be dealt with through a separate process, rather than simply being a minor setback issue. This approach can be seen in cases where the city has taken a more aggressive stance against non-conforming structures, viewing them as a threat to the overall character and integrity of the neighborhood.\n\nIn fact, there is a notable case from the City of Austin's Land Development Code Enforcement Division",
